detail.abstract:
Background: India accounts for the largest share of the global tuberculosis (TB) burden. Active Case Finding (ACF) approach, a systematic, proactive screening of high-risk populations, has been prioritised under the National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP). It aims to maximise TB screenings, to identify undiagnosed cases and accelerate progress toward India's 2025 TB elimination target. The Indian Council of Medical Research's (ICMR) 'Accelerating Efforts to End TB in India' project operationalises ACF across 24 districts, yet patient and frontline provider experiences of these interventions remain underexplored.&nbsp;
<div>
 Objective: This study explored patient and provider perspectives on ACF implementation in Palwal district, Haryana, to identify facilitators and barriers to participation and to derive recommendations for strengthening patient-centred TB screening under NTEP.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Methods: A Qualitative exploratory design was used, comprising in-depth interviews (IDIs) with 40 presumptive, diagnosed, and treated TB patients and 40 accompanying family members, four focus group discussions (FGDs) with ASHAs and ANMs (8-10 participants per FGD) and IDIs with ICMR project and programme staff, state and district TB staff, including District TB Officers (n=10-12). Interviews were conducted in Hindi, translated and analysed thematically following Braun and Clarke's six-step framework.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Results: The Active Case Finding(ACF) intervention enhanced access to diagnosis and facilitated early referrals, by means of door to door screenings while challenges such as workforce pressure, supply chain disruptions and inadequate feedback mechanisms hindered overall effectiveness to some extent. Across two ACF rounds, refusal rates for sputum collection remained high (32.5% and 29.9% respectively), reflecting a persistent trust deficit despite expanded screening coverage. Key facilitators included empathetic, community-fluent frontline health workers and the introduction of decentralised molecular diagnostics. Key barriers included TB-related stigma, particularly affecting young and unmarried women, absence of formal documentation for individuals testing negative, inconsistent medicine supply, delayed health worker incentives and limited institutionalised coordination between ICMR and NTEP structures.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Conclusion: Although, technological infrastructure and increased screening coverage resulting from the ACF intervention are critical components to acheive tuberculosis (TB) elimination, they alone are insufficient and it is imperative to engage in fostering community trust, enhancing support for frontline healthcare workers and ensuring transparency in screening processes.
</div>

Purpose – This study conceptualizes politically motivated consumer boycotts (PMCBs) as a distinct form of political consumerism, triggered by brands’ alignment with political ideologies, actors, or events—particularly in polarized societies. Drawing on political consumerism and social movement theory, it explores the sociopolitical conditions under which PMCBs emerge and the unique motivations behind consumer participation.&nbsp;
<div>
 Design/methodology/approach – Adopting a qualitative case study approach, the research draws on non-participant observation, semi-structured interviews with 16 boycott participants, social media posts, and relevant secondary sources. Data collection spanned three months, from April 18 to July 21, 2017, following a high-profile boycott in Turkey.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Findings – In politically polarized contexts, consumers initiate boycotts in response to perceived political alignment by brands—not to demand change, but to express dissent, signal group identity, and deter similar behavior from other companies. These boycotts are emotionally charged, resistant to brand reconciliation, and largely unaffected by personal cost. Political identity and polarization, along with macro-structural dynamics and meso-level mobilization through digital networks, serve as critical drivers of participation.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Originality/value – This study expands the boycott repertoire by introducing PMCB as a concept rooted in political identity and affective polarization. It contributes a multi-level understanding of boycott emergence—bridging political consumerism with social movement theory—and refines existing motivational typologies within consumer activism research.
</div>
